window.docContents[72].resolve({"/IndProp/#Logical-Foundations--IndProp___-Inductively-Defined-Propositions--Inductively-Defined-Propositions--Example___-Permutations":{"contents":"The familiar mathematical concept of permutation also has an\nelegant formulation as an inductive relation.  For simplicity,\nlet's focus on permutations of lists with exactly three\nelements.\n\nWe can define such permutations by the following rules:\n\n   ───────────────────────── (swap12)\n   Perm3 [a, b, c] [b, a, c]\n\n   ───────────────────────── (swap23)\n   Perm3 [a, b, c] [a, c, b]\n\nPerm3 l₁ l₂       Perm3 l₂ l₃\n───────────────────────────── (trans)\n         Perm3 l₁ l₃\n\n\nFor instance we can derive Perm3 [1, 2, 3] [3, 2, 1] as follows:\n\n───────────────────────── (swap12)  ─────────────────────── (swap23)\nPerm3 [1, 2, 3] [2, 1, 3]            Perm3 [2, 1, 3] [2, 3, 1]\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────(trans)    ───────────────────── (swap12)\nPerm3 [1, 2, 3] [2, 3, 1]                                                    Perm3 [2, 3, 1] [3, 2, 1]\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── (trans)\nPerm3 [1, 2, 3] [3, 2, 1]\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn Lean, we can define Perm3 as follows:\n\ninductive Perm3 {α : Type} : List α → List α → Prop where\n  | swap12 {x y z : α} : Perm3 [x, y, z] [y, x, z]\n  | swap23 {x y z : α} : Perm3 [x, y, z] [x, z, y]\n  | trans {l₁ l₂ l₃ : List α}\n    (h₁₂ : Perm3 l₁ l₂)\n    (h₂₃ : Perm3 l₂ l₃) :\n    Perm3 l₁ l₃\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009IndProp: Inductively Defined Propositions\u0009Inductively Defined Propositions","header":"9.1.5. Example: Permutations","id":"/IndProp/#Logical-Foundations--IndProp___-Inductively-Defined-Propositions--Inductively-Defined-Propositions--Example___-Permutations"},"/Logic/#Logical-Foundations--Logic-in-Lean--The-Logic-of-Lean--Functional-Extensionality--Other-Extensionality-Principles":{"contents":"\n\nWe can use ext on pairs as:\n\nexample {n : Nat} {p : Nat × Nat} (hx_fst : p.fst = n + 1) (hx_snd : p.snd = 0) :\n    (n + 1, 0) = p := by\n  ext -- uses the `Prod.ext` lemma\n  · rw [hx_fst]\n  · rw [hx_snd]\n\n\nNow, use ext1 to prove the following.\nRemember that dsimp only simplifies projections like (a, b).fst to a.example {m : Nat} {p : Nat × Nat} (hp_snd : p.snd = 4) (hp_fst : p.fst = m) :\n    ((p.fst + 1, 2), (p.fst, 4)) = ((m + 1, p.snd - 2), p) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Logic in Lean\u0009The Logic of Lean\u0009Functional Extensionality","header":"8.8.2.1. Other Extensionality Principles","id":"/Logic/#Logical-Foundations--Logic-in-Lean--The-Logic-of-Lean--Functional-Extensionality--Other-Extensionality-Principles"}});